As, rms velocity of ideal gas particles is given,

\begin{array}{rlrl}
\Rightarrow \quad v_{\mathrm{rms}} & =\sqrt{\frac{3 k T}{m}} \\
& v_{\mathrm{rms}} & \propto \sqrt{T}
\end{array}

where, temperature $T$ is measured in kelvin scale. So, the ratio

\frac{v_{1 \mathrm{rms}}^2}{v_{2 \mathrm{rms}}^2}=\frac{T_1}{T_2}

As given, $v_{2 \mathrm{rms}}=2 v_{1 \mathrm{rms}}$ and $T_1=27^{\circ} \mathrm{C}=300 \mathrm{~K}$
\begin{array}{rlrl}
\Rightarrow & & \frac{v_{1 \mathrm{~ms}}^2}{4 v_{1 \mathrm{rms}}^2} & =\frac{300}{x} \\
\Rightarrow & x & =300 \times 4=1200 \mathrm{~K}
\end{array}

Hence, $1200 \mathrm{~K}=(1200-273)^{\circ} \mathrm{C}=927^{\circ} \mathrm{C}$
Hence, the correct option is (2).

For a polyatomic atom value of specific heat at constant volume, $C_V=(3+f) R$ where, $f=$ vibration degree of freedom and $R=$ gas constant.
Now,

\begin{aligned}
& C_p=C_V+R \\
& C_p=(3+f) R+R \\
& C_p=(4+f) R
\end{aligned}

Now, ratio of $C_p$ and $C_V$
\frac{C_p}{C_V}=\frac{4+f}{3+f}

Root mean square velocity of a gas at temperature $T$ is

v_{\mathrm{mms}}=\sqrt{\frac{3 R T}{M}}

So, $\quad v_{\text {rms }} \propto \sqrt{T}$
When temperature is $4 T$,

v_{\mathrm{rms}}^{\prime} \propto \sqrt{4 T} \text { or } v_{(\mathrm{mms})}^{\prime}=2 v_{\mathrm{rms}}

